00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:05.920
Hong Kong is a bastion of free expression, art culture, and commerce.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.960
While mainland China was being ravaged by Mao's ultural revolution in the 1960's,

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.280
Hong Kong was still a British colony and home to a radical experiment in free market capitalism.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:22.220
This thriving, bustling, dynamic city has been made possible by the free market,

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:24.500
indeed the freest market in the world.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:30.360
Britain returned Hong Kong to China today ending 156 years of colonial rule.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:33.860
By the time partial control was handed over to the Chinese government,

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:40.140
creating the one country two systems model, per capita wealth here had eclipsed that of even the UK.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.280
That experiment may be coming to an abrupt end.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.640
China's People's Congress adopted a national security law that would allow the mainland to clamp down

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:55.400
on civil liberties, destroying the political freedom that made this semi-autonomous city a desirable place to live.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:59.660
The US government has threatened to revoke Hong Kong's special trading partner status

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:03.020
and levy targeted sanctions to oppose Beijing's actions.

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:07.700
But there's another foreign policy lever the government could pull that's nonviolent, humane,

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:14.580
and beneficial to US citizens: make it easier for Hong Kongers to leave by welcoming them into America.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:19.520
Not only would it be good for them but
it would also be good for the West.

